  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Convolution of Two One-Sided Exponentials",
    "lesson_core_move": "Set up the convolution of two one-sided exponentials by translating their unit-step supports into causal overlap bounds, multiplying the exponential terms, and preparing the integral for evaluation.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of Two One-Sided Exponentials",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Convolution of Two One-Sided Exponentials",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When two one-sided exponentials are convolved, the first move is to set up the integral correctly. In the convolution integral, t is the output time, and $\\tau$ is the dummy variable where the two signals are compared.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case where both signals are causal exponentials. The constants a and b are positive, so the unit steps are what determine where each signal is turned on.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-at}u(t),\\qquad h(t)=e^{-bt}u(t),\\qquad a>0,\\ b>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Inside the convolution integral, rewrite the first signal as $x(\\tau)$ and the second signal as $h(t - \\tau)$. The unit-step arguments change in exactly the same way as the exponential arguments.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=e^{-a\\tau}u(\\tau),\\qquad h(t-\\tau)=e^{-b(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use the unit steps to find the overlap. The factor $u(\\tau)$ requires $\\tau$ to be at least 0. The factor $u(t - \\tau)$ requires t - $\\tau$ to be at least 0, which means $\\tau$ is at most t.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\nu(\\tau)&\\Rightarrow \\tau \\ge 0,\\\\\\nu(t-\\tau)&\\Rightarrow t-\\tau \\ge 0 \\Rightarrow \\tau \\le t\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both one-sided factors are nonzero only on the interval from 0 to t. That interval exists when t is nonnegative; when t is negative, there is no causal overlap.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0\\le \\tau \\le t\\qquad \\text{when }t\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On that overlap interval, multiply the exponential parts to form the integrand. The unit steps have already done their job by setting the bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-a\\tau}e^{-b(t-\\tau)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the setup for the convolution is the causal integral below. The factor $u(t)$ records that the output is zero before the two one-sided signals overlap. Evaluating this integral is a later step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=u(t)\\int_0^t e^{-a\\tau}e^{-b(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of Two One-Sided Exponentials",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Rewriting One-Sided Exponentials in the Convolution Variable",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $x(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$ and $h(t)=e$^{-3t}$u(t)$ as $x(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)$ inside the convolution integral.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the convolution product $x(\\tau)$$h(t - \\tau)$. Before finding bounds or multiplying exponentials, rewrite each original signal with the argument it has inside the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the original one-sided exponentials.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t),\\qquad h(t)=e^{-3t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the first factor, replace t by $\\tau$ everywhere in $x(t)$. The exponential argument and the unit-step argument both change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=e^{-2\\tau}u(\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the second factor, replace t by t - $\\tau$ everywhere in $h(t)$. Again, carry that same substitution into both the exponential and the unit step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t-\\tau)=e^{-3(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the two factors that enter the convolution setup are $x(\\tau)=e$^{-2$\\tau$}$u(\\tau)$ and $h(t - \\tau)=e$^{-3(t - $\\tau$)}$u(t - \\tau)$. The next step is to use the unit-step factors to find the overlap bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of Two One-Sided Exponentials",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Causal Overlap Bounds",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(\\tau)=e$^{-2$\\tau$}$u(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)=e$^{-3(t-$\\tau$)}$u(t-\\tau)$, find the $\\tau$-overlap bounds.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the pair of unit-step factors. The exponential factors affect the value of the integrand, but the unit steps decide where the product can be nonzero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=e^{-2\\tau}u(\\tau),\\qquad h(t-\\tau)=e^{-3(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First read the support condition from $u(\\tau)$. This factor is on when $\\tau$ is at least 0.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(\\tau)\\Rightarrow \\tau\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next read the support condition from $u(t - \\tau)$. This factor is on when its argument is at least 0.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t-\\tau)\\Rightarrow t-\\tau\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solve that second inequality for $\\tau$. Moving $\\tau$ to the other side gives the upper bound.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-\\tau\\ge 0\\Rightarrow \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now intersect the two support conditions. Both factors can be nonzero only when $\\tau$ is at least 0 and at most t.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0\\le \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This interval exists when t is nonnegative. If t is negative, the upper bound t is less than 0, so the two one-sided supports do not overlap.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{overlap bounds: }0\\le \\tau\\le t\\text{ for }t\\ge 0,\\qquad \\text{no overlap for }t<0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of Two One-Sided Exponentials",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Multiplying the Shifted Exponential Terms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(\\tau)=e$^{-2$\\tau$}$u(\\tau)$ and $h(t-\\tau)=e$^{-3(t-$\\tau$)}$u(t-\\tau)$, multiply the exponential terms inside the overlap interval.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the unit-step factors have already set the overlap interval. On that interval, the algebraic work is to multiply only the exponential parts and simplify the product.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2\\tau}e^{-3(t-\\tau)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the shifted exponential factor. Distribute the -3 across t - $\\tau$ so the t term and the $\\tau$ term are visible.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-3(t-\\tau)}=e^{-3t+3\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute that back into the product. Now both $\\tau$-dependent exponents are in the same expression, so they can be combined.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2\\tau}e^{-3(t-\\tau)}=e^{-2\\tau}e^{-3t+3\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When multiplying exponentials with the same base, add the exponents. The $\\tau$ terms combine as $-2\\tau + 3\\tau = \\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2\\tau}e^{-3t+3\\tau}=e^{-3t+\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "It is often useful to separate the t-only factor from the $\\tau$-dependent factor, because the next setup step will place this expression inside the overlap integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-3t+\\tau}=e^{-3t}e^{\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the simplified exponential part of the integrand is $e^{-3t}$$e^{\\tau}$ over the overlap interval $0 \\le \\tau \\le t$. This is only the integrand preparation, not the evaluated convolution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2\\tau}e^{-3(t-\\tau)}=e^{-3t}e^{\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of Two One-Sided Exponentials",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Writing the Convolution Integral for Two One-Sided Exponentials",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up, but do not evaluate, the convolution integral for $x(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$ and $h(t)=e$^{-3t}$u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that all three setup pieces are now available: the convolution form, the causal overlap bounds, and the exponential product. This section puts those pieces into one bounded integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First rewrite the two original one-sided exponentials in the arguments used inside convolution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=e^{-2\\tau}u(\\tau),\\qquad h(t-\\tau)=e^{-3(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next use the unit-step factors to restrict the integral. The factor $u(\\tau)$ gives $\\tau \\ge 0$, and the factor $u(t - \\tau)$ gives $\\tau \\le t$, so the overlap is from 0 to t when t is nonnegative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0\\le \\tau\\le t\\qquad \\text{for }t\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On that interval, the unit steps have already done their job. The integrand is the product of the two exponential parts.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2\\tau}e^{-3(t-\\tau)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now replace the infinite convolution limits with the overlap limits. Attach a causal $u(t)$ factor to record that there is no overlap, and therefore zero output, when t is negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=u(t)\\int_0^t e^{-2\\tau}e^{-3(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Using the simplified product from the previous step, the same setup can also be written with the simplified integrand. This is still only a setup; no antiderivative has been taken.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(x*h)(t)=u(t)\\int_0^t e^{-3t}e^{\\tau}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
